FAQ = [
 ("Does my Explorer already have a factory hitch?",
  "Look under the rear bumper for a square 2 in receiver and check the window sticker for a Trailer Tow Package. On 2020–2024 Explorers the package was optional, so many trucks left the factory with no receiver at all. Dealer and reference pages report that Ford made the Class III Trailer Tow Package standard on every 2025 trim (Active, ST-Line, ST, Platinum). If a receiver is already there, you don't need anything on this page except a ball mount and wiring."),
 ("How much can a 2020–2024 Explorer tow?",
  "With the tow package, the 2.3L EcoBoost four-cylinder is rated at 5,300 lb and the 3.0L EcoBoost V6 (ST, Platinum, King Ranch) at 5,600 lb. The hybrid carries a lower rating, and the sources we checked disagree on the exact number, so read the towing chart in your owner's guide for the hybrid. Without the factory package, Ford's full rating may not apply; the package also adds cooling and wiring, not just a receiver."),
 ("Does an aftermarket hitch give me the full 5,600 lb rating?",
  "Not by itself. The hitch has its own rating (6,000 lb for the CURT 13438) and the Explorer has its own, and you're limited to whichever is lower. Ford's highest ratings were quoted for trucks equipped with the factory tow package, which also covers cooling and wiring. If you plan to tow near 5,000 lb or more, ask a Ford dealer what your VIN is rated for without the package."),
 ("Why did 2025 ratings drop to 5,000 lb?",
  "Dealer pages and TowingSpecs list 5,000 lb for every 2025 and 2026 Explorer, regardless of engine, while 2020–2024 models were rated at 5,300 or 5,600 lb with the package. Ford hasn't explained the change on the pages we read. Use the number in your own owner's guide or on your door-jamb label; that is the one that applies to your truck, not a figure from a brochure or a forum post."),
 ("Do I have to cut the bumper to install a hitch?",
  "For the CURT 13438 (etrailer CU78FR), yes: etrailer's notes call for trimming the plastic underbody panel and the bumper fascia, modifying the heat shield and temporarily lowering the exhaust. No frame drilling is needed. Draw-Tite's pages for the 76910 Hidden Hitch and the 76320 don't mention fascia trimming, but always read the instructions for your part before you start. If you don't want to cut, a shop can do it in an afternoon."),
 ("Will a hitch interfere with the hands-free liftgate kick sensor?",
  "It can. etrailer says the CURT is a tight fit on Explorers with the kick sensor and that steps 5, 6 and 8 of the instructions cover it. The Blue Ox hitch on the same page is listed as not intended for models with the kick sensor. If your Explorer has the hands-free liftgate, pick a hitch whose instructions cover it."),
 ("What is the Draw-Tite Hidden Hitch and who should buy it?",
  "The 76910 has a removable 2 in receiver, so nothing hangs under the bumper when you're not using it. The trade-off is capacity: Draw-Tite rates it at 3,500 lb GTW and 350 lb tongue weight, well under the 6,000 lb of a standard Class III. It suits bike racks, cargo carriers and small trailers, not a 5,000 lb camper."),
 ("What wiring do I need?",
  "Explorers with the factory tow package already have trailer sockets. Without the package you need a vehicle-specific 4-way harness for lights, and a 7-way plus a brake controller if the trailer has electric brakes. Most camping trailers of 3,000 lb and up use electric brakes, so plan for the 7-way from the start if that's where you're headed."),
 ("Is a Class IV hitch better than a Class III on an Explorer?",
  "Only for tongue weight headroom. Draw-Tite's Class IV 76320 is rated 6,000 lb GTW like the CURT Class III, but 900 lb tongue weight instead of 600. The Explorer's own 5,300–5,600 lb limit doesn't change, so a Class IV mostly helps if you carry a heavy hitch-mounted cargo carrier or bike rack. For ordinary trailers, a Class III rated above the Explorer's limit is enough."),
 ("Does the same hitch fit the Lincoln Aviator?",
  "Yes, in most cases. The CURT 13438, Draw-Tite 76910 and Draw-Tite 76320 all list the 2020–2025 Aviator alongside the Explorer, since the two share the rear frame. Draw-Tite quotes a longer install for the 2026 Aviator. Check the listing for your exact model year. Budget listings on this page also name the Aviator, but confirm the Aviator's exhaust and fascia match what the seller's instructions show."),
]

